 - Round 29 finding 5 — `useAuth.jsx`'s `readStoredSession` (`useAuth.jsx:86`) has the same class of timestamp-coherence gap Round 29 closed for cctpTrack's `ts` field (`EscrowDetail.jsx`'s `isValidCctpTrackRecord` — see git history for the fix): `s.issuedAt` is only checked for truthiness (`if (!s.issuedAt) return null`), never for being finite or non-future. A non-finite `issuedAt` makes `Date.now() - s.issuedAt > SESSION_TTL_MS` evaluate to `false` (NaN comparisons are always false), and a future `issuedAt` makes the same subtraction negative — either way the session's own expiry check silently never fires. `readActivityAt` already guards its own value with `Number.isFinite`, but neither it nor `readStoredSession` reject a future timestamp, so the same defeat applies to the inactivity-ceiling check at `useAuth.jsx:104`. Out of scope for this PR (different subsystem: session auth, not confirm-descriptors or delivery-tracking); not fixed here.
+- Round 34 (PR #18, 22nd review pass) — `useAuth.jsx`'s `executeContractCall` (`useAuth.jsx:378-410`) has no single-flight guard around the shared `sdkRef` SDK singleton. Two components can each start a Circle transaction before the first one's confirm iframe is ready: the second call's `applyConfirmLocalization`/`sdk.execute()` overwrites the singleton's mutable challenge/descriptor/callback state, so the first action's frame can end up showing the second action's confirmation screen while the first action's promise never resolves. This matters more than an ordinary race because it undermines the confirm-descriptors' core guarantee (the screen shown matches the action being signed) via a timing window rather than a wording flaw — the exact property this whole descriptor sweep exists to protect. Confirmed pre-existing in the base branch (`feature/circle-ucw-wallets`), not introduced by any of the 33 rounds on `feature/confirm-descriptors`. Deferred rather than fixed here: (a) it predates everything this PR has touched, and (b) there's a concrete plan to replace Circle's popup entirely with a custom confirmation UI, which will restructure or eliminate this exact execution path — hardening code that's about to be replaced isn't worth it. Suggested fix for whoever picks this up (likely during the custom-UI work): a global in-flight guard in `AuthProvider`, held from before challenge creation until the SDK callback settles, so a second `executeContractCall` either queues or rejects instead of clobbering the first.
 
